1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,492
An agent doesn't only run from its own panel.

2
00:00:01,492 --> 00:00:04,243
It can be called mid-conversation, handed a subtask, and hand back just the result.

3
00:00:04,243 --> 00:00:06,000
Let's see that, and the memory it keeps between runs.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,727
First, turn on the Agents tool group inside a chat.

5
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:16,000
That's what lets the model in this conversation reach out and call one of your saved agents as a tool, instead of doing the work itself.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,696
Ask the chat to have the code-reviewer agent look at a specific file.

7
00:00:19,696 --> 00:00:28,000
It hands off the subtask, the agent runs its full spec, and only the result comes back — you see a tool-call card, not the agent's internal back-and-forth.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,139
Notice what's not in the main conversation: the agent's own tool calls, its reasoning, any files it opened along the way.

9
00:00:33,139 --> 00:00:34,965
All of that stays inside the delegated run.

10
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:39,000
Your chat only sees the finished output — which keeps the main conversation short and readable.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,410
Each agent also keeps its own memory, separate from chat, project, or global memory.

12
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:50,000
Run the code-reviewer twenty times and it accumulates what it's specifically learned about this codebase's conventions — that memory travels with the agent, not with any one conversation.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,000
A checklist for writing an agent worth delegating to: be specific about its scope, say exactly what output format you want back, give it the minimum tools it actually needs, and tell it what to do when it gets stuck instead of leaving it to guess.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,099
One agent, delegated to.

15
00:01:03,099 --> 00:01:08,000
Next, several agents working the same job at once — swarms — and when that's actually worth the extra cost.

